>I'd love to use it on my laptop, but before spending a bit under
>USD$200 for it, I wan't to _know_ that it will be able to boot 
>Debian off my external hard drive, and I don't find a writeup with
>that level of detail for it anywhere.

Short answer: Yes it will. 

Longer answer: VMware doesn't mind where the boot partition or
disk-image files are, as long as they're readable. To have a free play
with it, http://www.vmware.com/products/free_virtualization.html
download the VMWare Player and go have a look at the preconfigured
Virtual Appliances linked from that page (these are all disk-images,
of course); or download VMWare Server and make your own from the usual
installation media.

>I need to boot from my external drive because laptops come with the
>WinXP hard drive pre-configured, and no installation media, and thus
>with no safe way to repartition the drive to boot Linux from the
>internal hard drive.

Don't most Linux distros come with some sort of nondestructive
repartition tool these days, as part of the installer? If not, there's
always Partition Magic or Acronis.

And you don't need to use partitions anyway, you can use virtual disks
made in the filesystem of the host OS. All of my VMs are of this type.

ObOntopic: And then you can play Nethack in Linux under Windows.

